{"title":"2002 USAMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Lagrange_Multipliers)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Lagrange Multipliers)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> be a triangle such that\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/f/93f7adec454ebe970f7dd462f42f733efd6172ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left( \\cot \\frac{A}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( 2 \\cot \\frac{B}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( 3 \\cot \\frac{C}{2} \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{6s}{7r} \\right)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"384\" height=\"46\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> denote its <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Semiperimeter\" title=\"Semiperimeter\">semiperimeter</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inradius\" title=\"Inradius\">inradius</a>, respectively.  Prove that triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> is similar to a triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> whose side lengths are all positive integers with no common divisor and determine those integers.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b29b358e825defa3e11b7d903d43ec31e5909a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/e/d8efab0f68cfe83cd75f01f951920f544da89d49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC, CA, AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"99\" height=\"16\" />, respectively.  Since the line from a triangle's incenter to one of its vertices bisects the angle at the triangle's vertex, the condition of the problem is equivalent to the\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/767e8e5194e8327b7dc146f642b33ed128da9b24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left( \\frac{s-a}{r} \\right)^2 + 4\\left( \\frac{s-b}{r} \\right)^2 + 9\\left( \\frac{s-c}{r} \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{6s}{7r} \\right)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"377\" height=\"46\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>or\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/a/b3a9168af70b33efd101c18651d74ebc5afbe703.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(s-a)^2}{36} + \\frac{(s-b)^2}{9} + \\frac{(s-c)^2}{4} = \\frac{s^2}{36 + 9 + 4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"346\" height=\"40\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>But by the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cauchy-Schwarz_Inequality\" title=\"Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality\">Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality</a>, we know\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/f/8ff3702c4f3caf1928f0f6804aa07aebea95134a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{matrix} (36 + 9 + 4) \\left[ \\frac{(s-a)^2}{36} + \\frac{(s-b)^2}{9} + \\frac{(s-c)^2}{4} \\right] &amp; \\ge &amp;\\left[ (s-a) + (s-b) + (s-c) \\right]^2\\\\ &amp; = &amp; s^2 \\\\ \\qquad\\qquad \\quad \\quad \\frac{(s-a)^2}{36} + \\frac{(s-b)^2}{9} + \\frac{(s-c)^2}{4} &amp; \\ge &amp;\\frac{s^2}{36 + 9 + 4} \\; , \\end{matrix}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -35px\" width=\"558\" height=\"79\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>with equality only when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/4/13431a91a7610d3dfe2281c9bcd7c4812f9be77b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(s-a)^2}{36}, \\frac{(s-b)^2}{9}, \\frac{(s-c)^2}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"208\" height=\"39\" /> are directly proportional to 36, 9, 4, respectively.  Therefore (clearing denominators and taking square roots) our problem requires that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/0/59011b57a654b4ffbf813e5f2e2d798a610ddfe4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(s-a), (s-b), (s-c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"174\" height=\"18\" /> be directly proportional to 36, 9, 4, and since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/c/01cfcd878714e445c5fea7096c053ac003b7bcc4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = (s-b) + (s-c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"160\" height=\"18\" /> etc., this is equivalent to the condition that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/b/a5b29b358e825defa3e11b7d903d43ec31e5909a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b,c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"43\" height=\"18\" /> be in proportion with 13, 40, 45, Q.E.D.\n</p><p>Sidenote: A 13, 40, 45 obtuse triangle has an integer area of 252 and an inradius of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/7/01728334aa0fa74c7d25ec83f9c668e98c358430.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{36}{7}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Lagrange_Multipliers.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Lagrange_Multipliers)\">Solution 2 (Lagrange Multipliers)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Lagrange Multipliers)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/8/c18e240b7c7dbc4b2e6da70cd13ba155dc475c0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D, E, F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" /> be the projections of the incenter onto the sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, respectively, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/9/3597be581068a0bf584794a545dc02c40abdc61d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=AF . y=BD, z=CE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"202\" height=\"18\" />. We thus have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/6/1e6413aab32fe386b28fa45a6d5fd79f3af4eb8c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\cot \\frac{A}{2} = \\frac{x}{r} , \\cot \\frac{B}{2} = \\frac{y}{r} , \\cot \\frac{C}{2} = \\frac{z}{r}\\]\" width=\"264\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>and additionally, \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/d/f5df28c4ee93512342b1b995cb11ced1b320d3f3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x+y+z = s\\]\" width=\"105\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/9/cf9d735f5858d65d8c66c04645e5ff7019da59bc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x^2 + 4 y^2 + 9 z^2 = (\\frac{6s}{7})^2\\]\" width=\"179\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>We first determine the side lengths of the triangle similar to this triangle with semiperimeter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. It is asked of us to show that such a triangle is unique, but we note that this is true if and only if the plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/c/0fce09322356796e6adce205b1e7d6d0b7d7e5ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"15\" /> and the ellipsoid <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/3/0731dfd734b69433e732caa272e11e2e58b932a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + 4 y^2 + 9 z^2 = (\\frac{6}{7})^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"175\" height=\"38\" /> is tangent. This can be shown if we use the method of Lagrange Multipliers on the constraint <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/c/0fce09322356796e6adce205b1e7d6d0b7d7e5ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"15\" /> and function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/f/bff4c739decf40a77af216c26b49af9b2e747fd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + 4 y^2 + 9 z^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"116\" height=\"20\" /> to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/b/24b2511ef1a9e8ece44116a66e51570badb3d387.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{6}{7})^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"36\" height=\"38\" /> is a extrema.\n</p><p>The Method of Lagrange Multipliers gives the system of \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/8/55836926766a2be1d3133308034c0a2074c8afc6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2x= \\lambda\\]\" width=\"53\" height=\"12\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/1/b01dcf862c9839356d855d83c1e532490c04bef9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[8y= \\lambda\\]\" width=\"52\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/4/2e4af89d71dcd815d00fe06eda1a633623168725.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[18z= \\lambda\\]\" width=\"60\" height=\"12\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/2/c621318c449929f0901592d85f0d545a3f543215.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x+y+z=1\\]\" width=\"105\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>from which we easily obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/4/cd401ab46cf0cfa2ebbbb4886976249ffd39b935.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y,z)=(\\frac{36}{49} , \\frac{9}{49} , \\frac{4}{49} )$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"182\" height=\"38\" />. We calculate that this indeed satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/3/0731dfd734b69433e732caa272e11e2e58b932a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + 4 y^2 + 9 z^2 = (\\frac{6}{7})^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"175\" height=\"38\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/b/24b2511ef1a9e8ece44116a66e51570badb3d387.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{6}{7})^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"36\" height=\"38\" /> is indeed an relative extrema. To check that it is a global extrema, one notes that as x, y, or z goes towards plus or minus infinity, the function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/f/bff4c739decf40a77af216c26b49af9b2e747fd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2 + 4 y^2 + 9 z^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"116\" height=\"20\" /> goes to positive infinity, so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/b/24b2511ef1a9e8ece44116a66e51570badb3d387.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{6}{7})^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"36\" height=\"38\" /> is the global minima. Now, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/b/33bdc9e2425b754c8bd49a1fc5c86c567f354dd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=x+y=\\frac{45}{49}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"143\" height=\"38\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/c/49c0e99e29ef3de3223a1d437bd01e368a4d4dd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC=x+z=\\frac{40}{49}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"142\" height=\"38\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/a/d8a5c89e223be2b2660832271fd70ef78088b09c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC=y+z=\\frac{13}{49}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"143\" height=\"38\" />, so we see that the desired side lengths are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/a/6aa66f4a47486da5185544680b33b6bb3641f94b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=45, AC=40, BC=13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"231\" height=\"18\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ddk001\" title=\"Ddk001\">Ddk001</a>\n</p><p><br />\n<i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO\" title=\"2002 USAMO\">2002 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2002 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2002\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2002 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2002 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2002 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2002 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2002 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2002 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2002_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2002 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017020627\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.038 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.059 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 277/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1663/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 48/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8711/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   17.973      1 -total\n 39.12%    7.032      1 Template:USAMO_newbox\n 29.86%    5.366      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n 29.56%    5.312      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!--